<BaseLayout title="Plants We Met — Huanyu Chen"
  description="The trees and flowers that stood out on our trips, 2022–2026: the Grizzly Giant and the redwoods, a monkeypod at the Honolulu airport, two ponytail palms, saguaros and Joshua trees, bougainvillea in Sanya and Tempe, a flame tree, plumeria and hibiscus in Hawaiʻi, angel's trumpets in Santa Barbara, wisteria in Solvang and red maples in Whistler.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022 – 2026</p>
  <h1>Plants We Met</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Giant trees, strange shapes, desert plants and tropical flowers from our trips.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>5 years · May 2022 – September 2026</strong><br />
    a giant sequoia and a redwood forest · a monkeypod at an airport · two ponytail palms · a row of very tall pines ·
    a saguaro · Joshua trees by day and by night · a cactus garden on a campus · aloes in bloom · bougainvillea in two
    countries · hibiscus and plumeria · a flame tree and a shower tree in bloom · leaves as big as umbrellas · angel's trumpets · wisteria · red maples · a
    golden tree · a garden of roses
  </div>

  <p>
    Our walks around Los Angeles have their own post (<a href="/misc/la-gardens">Gardens, a Getty and a
    Battleship</a>). This one is about the plants themselves: the ones on trips that made us stop, because they were
    enormous, oddly shaped, or simply nothing like what grows at home. We are not botanists, and a few of them we
    still can't name. Each one links to the trip it came from.
  </p>

  <h2 id="giants">Giants</h2>
  <p>
    The biggest living things we have stood next to are the giant sequoias of the <strong>Mariposa Grove</strong> in
    Yosemite. On Labor Day weekend in 2022 we walked up to the Grizzly Giant, one of the oldest trees in the grove,
    its trunk split and fire-blackened at the base (<a href="/misc/yosemite">Yosemite</a>). Their cousins,
    the coast redwoods, are taller and slimmer. In August 2026 Wangcai posed on a fallen one at
    <strong>Henry Cowell Redwoods</strong> near Santa Cruz.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sequoia-mariposa.webp'} alt="The Grizzly Giant, a massive giant sequoia with a red-brown fire-scarred trunk and twisted upper branches, with visitors small at its base." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Grizzly Giant, September 4, 2022.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'redwoods-santa-cruz.webp'} alt="A white dog standing on a fallen redwood log in a forest of tall, straight redwood trunks." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Redwoods near Santa Cruz, August 11, 2026.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A giant of a different shape waits at the <strong>Honolulu airport</strong>, in the open-air gardens between the
    terminals: a monkeypod tree, low and impossibly wide, its branches spreading over the paths like an umbrella
    (<a href="/misc/hawaii-2026">Hawaiʻi, 2026</a>).
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'monkeypod-honolulu.webp'} alt="A huge monkeypod tree with a short trunk and long, wide, horizontal branches spreading over an airport garden." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A monkeypod at the Honolulu airport, September 10, 2026.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="shapes">Strange shapes</h2>
  <p>
    Twice in Hawaiʻi there was a tree that looked like a giant green mop: a swollen trunk topped with a huge,
    shaggy head of long, thin leaves. As far as we can tell, they are ponytail palms, which are not palms at all but
    a relative of the agave. The first was on a roundabout on Waikoloa Beach Drive on my 2025 trip
    (<a href="/misc/big-island-2025">Big Island, 2025</a>), the second on a lawn in Princeville on Kauaʻi
    (<a href="/misc/kauai-2026">Kauaʻi, 2026</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ponytail-waikoloa.webp'} alt="A large tree with a thick, swollen trunk and a round, drooping crown of long thin leaves on a roundabout, with palms behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Waikoloa, September 11, 2025.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ponytail-princeville.webp'} alt="A huge mop-shaped tree with a thick trunk and a dense, shaggy crown of long leaves on a lawn, with mountains and parked cars behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Princeville, Kauaʻi, April 24, 2026.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Also in Princeville, the road into town is lined with pines so tall and narrow they look drawn with a ruler. They
    are most likely Cook pines, which are planted all over Hawaiʻi and tend to lean slightly toward the equator.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pines-princeville.webp'} alt="A row of very tall, narrow, columnar pine trees around a roundabout with a fountain, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tall pines in Princeville, April 22, 2026.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="desert">Desert</h2>
  <p>
    The desert plants were the most foreign of all. At the <strong>Desert Botanical Garden</strong> in Phoenix in March
    2025, the saguaros stood with their arms up along the paths, with Wangcai in front (<a href="/misc/phoenix-2025">Phoenix,
    2025</a>). A month later at the San Diego Zoo <strong>Safari Park</strong>, a bed of aloes was in full bloom, red
    spikes rising out of the spiky rosettes (<a href="/misc/san-diego">San Diego</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'saguaro-phoenix.webp'} alt="A tall saguaro cactus with raised arms above desert shrubs and smaller cacti along a sandy path, a dog's ears in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A saguaro in Phoenix, March 22, 2025.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'aloes-safari-park.webp'} alt="Aloe plants with tall red flower spikes rising from spiky rosettes in a garden bed." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Aloes in bloom at the Safari Park, April 20, 2025.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Closer to home, the Joshua trees of Joshua Tree are not trees either but giant yuccas, and they look even
    stranger at night. In December 2024 we saw them both ways (<a href="/misc/joshua-tree">Joshua Tree</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'joshua-trees.webp'} alt="A wide desert plain of low shrubs and scattered Joshua trees, with boulders and mountains under a blue sky with streaks of cloud." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Joshua trees by day, December 9, 2024.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'joshua-tree-night.webp'} alt="A single Joshua tree lit up yellow against a black, starry night sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>And by night.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And the most unexpected desert of all is on a college campus. Stanford's <strong>Arizona Cactus Garden</strong>,
    laid out in the 1880s, still has barrel cacti, agaves and tall columnar cacti along its sandy paths
    (<a href="/misc/roadtrips-2022">the 2022 road trips</a>).
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stanford-cactus.webp'} alt="A garden of barrel cacti, tall columnar cacti and agaves along a curving sandy path at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Arizona Cactus Garden, May 20, 2022.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="tropical">Tropical</h2>
  <p>
    Bougainvillea is the flower of warm places, and we found it in two countries: spilling over a hillside above the
    sea in <strong>Sanya</strong> on Hainan Island in February 2023 (<a href="/misc/china-2023">China, 2023</a>), and
    in a bright pink bush beside a street in <strong>Tempe</strong> in March 2025.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bougainvillea-sanya.webp'} alt="Magenta bougainvillea on a hillside in the foreground, with a bay, a beach and a city below under white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bougainvillea above the sea, Sanya, February 9, 2023.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bougainvillea-tempe.webp'} alt="A bright pink and coral bougainvillea bush under a pale-barked tree, with a concrete building behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>And in Tempe, March 21, 2025.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In Hawaiʻi the flowers are everywhere. A red hibiscus, the classic Hawaiian flower, was blooming on a bush in
    Princeville, and a plumeria tree in Waikoloa had its clusters of white and yellow flowers, the ones that are
    strung into leis.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hibiscus-kauai.webp'} alt="Bright red hibiscus flowers on a leafy bush on a lawn, with tall pines behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hibiscus, Princeville, April 24, 2026.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'plumeria-waikoloa.webp'} alt="A plumeria tree with thick rosettes of long glossy leaves and clusters of white flowers with yellow centers, above orange heliconia." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Plumeria, Waikoloa, September 8, 2026.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The best flowering trees were on my 2025 trip, on the grounds of the <strong>Mauna Lani</strong> on the Kohala
    Coast, one September morning. One was covered in clusters of red-orange flowers above feathery leaves and long
    dark seed pods: a royal poinciana, also called a flame tree, with pink plumeria growing beside it. A little
    further on, a white plumeria tree was in full bloom against a wall of lava rock
    (<a href="/misc/big-island-2025">Big Island, 2025</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'flame-tree-mauna-lani.webp'} alt="A spreading tree with clusters of bright red-orange flowers and feathery green leaves over a garden path, with pink-flowered plumeria trees to the left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A flame tree, Mauna Lani, September 13, 2025.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'plumeria-mauna-lani.webp'} alt="A plumeria tree covered in white flowers above a lava-rock wall, under a light-green feathery tree and a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>White plumeria, the same morning.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The day before, in Waikoloa, there was one more: a tree hung with drooping clusters of flowers that shade from
    deep pink to peach and white. It was most likely a shower tree, pink or rainbow, one of the most common flowering
    trees in Hawaiʻi; the rainbow shower is Honolulu's official tree. This one had been pruned and was only partly in
    flower. The most spectacular ones, covered in pink from top to bottom, did not make it into the camera roll.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'shower-tree-waikoloa.webp'} alt="The crown of a tree with gray branches and pinnate leaves, hung with drooping clusters of pink, peach and white flowers against a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A shower tree, Waikoloa, September 12, 2025.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the coffee farm in Holualoa, above Kona, some of the leaves were as big as umbrellas: heart-shaped, with pale
    veins, growing out of the rocks.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'leaves-kona.webp'} alt="Huge heart-shaped green leaves with pale veins growing among rocks and smaller plants." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Giant leaves in Holualoa, September 9, 2026.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="color">Color</h2>
  <p>
    On a June afternoon in <strong>Santa Barbara</strong> in 2024, two striking plants were growing
    a few blocks apart downtown: angel's trumpets, with long yellow flowers hanging straight down like bells, and a vine
    covered in orange trumpet-shaped flowers whose name we still don't know (<a href="/misc/coast-weekends">Coast
    Weekends</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'angels-trumpet.webp'} alt="A shrub hung with many long, pale yellow trumpet-shaped flowers pointing downward." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Angel's trumpets, Santa Barbara, June 5, 2024.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'orange-vine.webp'} alt="A leafy vine covered in clusters of bright orange tubular flowers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An orange-flowered vine, the same afternoon.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In <strong>Solvang</strong> in March 2023, a pergola was hung with wisteria in full purple bloom. In
    <strong>Whistler</strong> that October, the maples around a small pond had turned bright red, with their leaves
    carpeting the ground (<a href="/misc/vancouver-2023">Vancouver, 2023</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wisteria-solvang.webp'} alt="Purple wisteria hanging from a white wooden pergola over a sidewalk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wisteria, Solvang, March 26, 2023.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'maples-whistler.webp'} alt="Bright red maple trees around a small rocky pond, with red leaves covering the ground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Red maples, Whistler, October 1, 2023.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two more from the same years. At <strong>UBC</strong> in Vancouver, a big tree had turned completely golden at
    the end of September. And in <strong>Portland</strong> in July 2023, the International Rose Test Garden was in
    full bloom (<a href="/misc/pnw-2023">the Pacific Northwest trip</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'golden-tree-ubc.webp'} alt="A large spreading tree with golden-yellow foliage on a university plaza under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A golden tree at UBC, September 29, 2023.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'roses-portland.webp'} alt="A dense bush of peach and orange roses in full bloom." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Roses in Portland, July 22, 2023.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <p class="muted"><small>Photo locations have been removed from the published images. Plant names are our best
    guesses where we weren't sure.</small></p>
